\item Consider the data set from the previous problem. 
Assume that the vector of daily log-returns $r \stackrel{d}=\mu + T$,
where $\mu = \overline r$ is the vector of sample means and $T$ is
distributed according to the $t$-distribution found in the previous problem.\\

{\bf (a)} Given a target expected daily return level $v$, determine the 
portflio weights $w_i,\ i=1,\dots,30$ such that $\sum_{i=1}^{30} w_i =1$, which
{\em minimize} VaR$_{0.95}$ of the portfolio return
$$
r(w):= w_1 r_1 + \cdots + w_{30} r_{30},
$$
where $r_i$ is the daily log-return of the $i$th stock in DJIA.
Write an $R$-function that returns the optimal portfolio weights
given a value for the target expected return.

{\bf How do the optimal portfolio weights for a given target expected return 
depend on the VaR level $\alpha$?  Explain.}

Consider $\sigma_w^2 := w^\top\Lambda w$. Then, by the hint, we know that:
\begin{align*}
    r(w) & \stackrel{d}{=} w^\top \mu + (w^\top \Lambda w)^{1/2} T_\nu \\
    & \stackrel{d}{=} w^\top \mu + \sigma_w T_\nu
\end{align*}
Thus, the Value-at-Risk must satisfy:
\begin{align*}
    \P(r(w) < -VaR_\alpha) &= \P(w^\top \mu + \sigma_w T_\nu < -VaR_\alpha)\\
    &= \P(T_\nu < \frac{-VaR_\alpha-w^\top \mu}{\sigma_w }) = 1-\alpha \\
    \Rightarrow \frac{-VaR_\alpha-w^\top \mu}{\sigma_w } &= q_{1-\alpha}(\nu) \\
    \Rightarrow VaR_\alpha &=  -w^\top \mu - (w^\top \Lambda w)^{1/2} q_{1-\alpha}(\nu)
\end{align*}
For a given target expected return, we have that $w^\top \mu = v$. Consequently, for a fixed VaR level, minimizing the Value-at-Risk is equivalent to minimizing the variance $\sigma_w^2$. In conclusion, we have the minimization problem:
\begin{equation}
\begin{array}{rrclcl}
\displaystyle \min_{w} & \multicolumn{3}{l}{w^\top\Lambda w}\\
\textrm{s.t.} & w^\top \mu = v  \\
&\sum_i w_i = 1
\end{array}
\end{equation}
Additionally, since $\Lambda = \frac{\nu-2}{\nu}\Sigma$, we can change the objective function to $w^\top\Sigma w$. Then, the objective function becomes the portfolio variance.

<<problem 2a, include=T>>=
library("quadprog");
mu = apply(ret,2,mean);
Sigma = cov(ret)
run_solve_QF <- function(Sigma, mu, v, short_sell = F) {
  d <- length(mu)
  Dmat <- 2 * Sigma  # Required because solve.QP uses (1/2)x'Dx
  dvec <- rep(0, d)
  Amat <- cbind(mu, rep(1, d))
  bvec <- c(v, 1)
  if (short_sell){
      Amat = cbind(Amat, diag(d))
      bvec = c(bvec, rep(0, d))
  }
  res <- solve.QP(Dmat = Dmat,
                  dvec = dvec,
                  Amat = Amat,
                  bvec = bvec,
                  meq = 2)
  return(list(variance = res$value, solution = res$solution))
}
@

In conclusion, the minimization of Value-at-Risk depends on the minimization of a quadratic form, and the weights affect the VaR only through this quadratic form that is associated with the variance of the portfolio.

{\bf (b)} Using the function in part {\bf (a)} compute the efficient frontier of
portfolios corresponding to several target daily log-return levels
$v=0.001/365$ to $v=0.65/365$. Graph the points $(SD(r(w)),\E (r(w)))$, 
corresponding to these portfolia. That is, produce a risk-reward diagram for 
the portfolios, where $SD(r(w))$ and $\E(r(w))$ denote the standard deviation 
and expectation of the returns, respectively. Assuming that the 
{\bf yearly} risk-free interest rate is $\mu_{F} = 0.03$, determine the ``tangency" portfolio, 
i.e., the one maximizing the Sharpe ratio $(\E r(w)-\mu_{F})/SD(r(w)).$ 
(Note: remember to work in the correct units -- i.e. determine the {\bf daily} risk-free rate. You
may also have to find an appropriate range of target expected returns in order to find the global 
maximum of the Sharpe ratio.)

<<problem 2b, include=T>>=
v.range = seq(from=0.001,to=0.75,by=0.001)/365;
sig.pf = matrix(0,nrow=length(v.range),ncol=1);
weights = matrix(0,nrow=length(v.range),ncol=30);
for (i in (1:length(v.range))){
 v = v.range[i];
 res = run_solve_QF(Sigma, mu, v, short_sell=F)
 sig.pf[i] = sqrt(res$variance);
 weights[i,] = res$solution;
}
mu_f = log(1.03)/365; # Risk-free rate
plot(sig.pf, v.range, type='l', col='blue',
     xlab="Std. Deviation Portfolio", ylab='Expected Return',
     main = "Risk-Reward Diagram")
sharp.ratio = (v.range - mu_f)/sig.pf
opt.sr.idx = which.max(sharp.ratio)
optimal.pf.short = c(v.range[opt.sr.idx], sig.pf[opt.sr.idx])
library(knitr)
kable(weights[opt.sr.idx, ])
@

The column above represents the weights of the tangencial portfolio (i.e. maximizes Sharpe ratio). We can observe that some of the weights are negative. This indicates that short-selling is allowed.

