{\bf (c)} Repeat part {\bf (b)} with a suitably chosen range of target expected returns
under the condition that no short-selling is allowed (i.e., the weights are all non-negative). 
Comment on the optimal portfolio weights in the two cases, i.e., with and without short-selling.  
Is the expected return in part {\bf (b)} sustainable/reasonable? 

<<problem 2c, include=T>>=
v.range = seq(from=0.001,to=0.75,by=0.001)/365;
v.range = v.range[v.range<max(mu)]
sig.pf = matrix(0,nrow=length(v.range),ncol=1);
weights = matrix(0,nrow=length(v.range),ncol=30);
for (i in (1:length(v.range))){
 v = v.range[i];
 res = run_solve_QF(Sigma, mu, v, short_sell=T)
 sig.pf[i] = sqrt(res$variance);
 weights[i,] = res$solution;
}
mu_f = log(1.03)/365; # Risk-free rate
plot(sig.pf, v.range, type='l', col='blue',
     xlab="Std. Deviation Portfolio", ylab='Expected Return',
     main = "Risk-Reward Diagram")
sharp.ratio = (v.range - mu_f)/sig.pf
opt.sr.idx = which.max(sharp.ratio)
optimal.pf.long = c(v.range[opt.sr.idx], sig.pf[opt.sr.idx])
library(knitr)
kable(weights[opt.sr.idx, ])
kable(rbind(optimal.pf.short, optimal.pf.long), 
      col.names = c("Expected Return", "Std. Deviation"))
@

In the case with short-selling, the expected return is 0.17\% and the standard deviation is 1.8\%. In the case without short-selling, the expected return is 0.09\% and the standard deviation is 1.1\%.

We can notice that the case that allows short-selling almost doubles the expected return of the other case. If we observe the weights of the optimal portfolio in (b), we note that the third stock has a strong short position of 33\%. Thus, short-selling allows the portfolio to invest more resources on high-yield stocks, and at the same time, it can profit from stocks that have losses. In conclusion, the expected return of portfolio (b) is only sustainable while there are companies experiencing losses.

\item Problem 6 on page 513 of the textbook \href{https://www.dropbox.com/s/0p7sz2oi0mgf7zn/Ruppert_Matheson_Statistics%20and%20Data%20Analysis%20for%20Financial%20Engineering.pdf?dl=0}{(link to the textbook)}.

(a) The value for the beta of stock A can be calculated from:
\begin{align*}
    \beta_A &= \frac{\sigma_{AM}}{\sigma_{M}^2} \\
    &= \frac{165 \%^2}{11^2 \%^2} \\
    &= 1.364
\end{align*}

This value of beta implies an "agressive" stock. 

(b) The expected return can be calculated from:
\begin{align*}
    \mu_A &= \mu_f + \beta_A (\mu_M - \mu_f) \\
    &= 4\% +  1.364\cdot (12\% - 4\%)\\
    &= 14.91\%
\end{align*}

(c) Finally, the percentage of the variance due to market risk is:
\begin{align*}
    \frac{\beta_A^2\, \sigma_M^2}{\sigma_A^2} 
    &= \frac{1.364^2\, 11^2 \%^2}{320\%^2} \\
    &= 0.7031
\end{align*}
In conclusion, the percentage of variance due to market risk is 70.31\%.

\item Problem 7 on page 513 of the textbook.

(a) The beta of the portfolio is the average of the given betas.
\begin{align*}
    \beta_P &= \frac{\beta_1+\beta_2+\beta_3}{3} \\
    &= 0.8667
\end{align*}
(b) The requested variance satisfies:
\begin{align*}
    \textrm{Var}(R_P-\mu_{ft}) 
    &= (\beta_P)^2 \, \textrm{Var}(R_{Mt}-\mu_{ft}) + \frac{1}{3}\sum_j \textrm{Var}(\epsilon_j) \\
    &= 0.8667^2 \cdot 0.014 + \frac{0.010+0.015+0.011}{3} \\
    &= 0.02252
\end{align*}
(c) We calculate the percentage as:
\begin{align*}
    \frac{(\beta_P)^2 \, \textrm{Var}(R_{Mt}-\mu_{ft})}{0.2252}
    = 0.4670
\end{align*}
Thus, the percentage of the risk due to market risk is 46.7\%.


\item Problem 2 on page 356 in the textbook (read Problem 1 terein on how to get the data).

<<problem 5a, include=TRUE>>=
library(Ecdat)
data(CRSPday)
crsp = CRSPday[,7]

ar1 = arima(crsp, order=c(1,0,0))
ar2 = arima(crsp, order=c(2,0,0))
selection = data.frame(
  Model = c("AR(1)", "AR(2)"),
  AIC = c(ar1$aic, ar2$aic),
  BIC = c(BIC(ar1), BIC(ar2))
)
library(knitr)
kable(selection)
@
We can observe that both AIC and BIC are lower for the model AR(1) compared to AR(2). Then, AR(1) is preferred. 

(b) We calculate the 95\%-confidence interval from the previous model.
<<problem 5b, include=TRUE>>=
phi.hat = as.numeric(ar1$coef[1])
se.phi = sqrt(ar1$var.coef[1,1])
ci = data.frame(
  Parameter = c("phi"),
  Estimate = c(phi.hat),
  LowerBound = c(phi.hat-1.96*se.phi),
  UpperBound = c(phi.hat+1.96*se.phi)
)
kable(ci)
@
Then, the 95\% CI for $\phi$ is $[0.0465, 0.1241]$.


\item Problem 4 on page 356 in the textbook.

(a) The variance of the $Y_t$ process is independent of time. It can be calculated from:
\begin{align*}
    \textrm{Var}(Y_1) &= \gamma(0) = \frac{\sigma_\epsilon^2}{1-\phi^2}  \\
    &= \frac{1.2}{1-0.4^2} = 1.4286
\end{align*}

(b) Now, we calculate the covariances:
\begin{align*}
    \textrm{Cov}(Y_1, Y_2) &= \gamma(1) =  \phi\cdot \gamma(0) \\
    &= 0.4 \cdot 1.4286 = 0.5714 \\
    \textrm{Cov}(Y_1, Y_3) &= \gamma(2) =  \phi^2\cdot \gamma(0) \\
    &= 0.4^2 \cdot 1.4286 = 0.2286
\end{align*}
(c) Finally, we calculate the requested variance:
\begin{align*}
    \textrm{Var}(0.5\,(Y_1+Y_2+Y_3)) &= 0.5^2\textrm{Cov}(Y_1+Y_2+Y_3, Y_1+Y_2+Y_3) \\
    &= 0.25 \;[\textrm{Var}(Y_1) + \textrm{Var}(Y_2) + \textrm{Var}(Y_3) \\
    & + 2\, (\textrm{Cov}(Y_1, Y_3)+\textrm{Cov}(Y_1, Y_2) + \textrm{Cov}(Y_2, Y_3))] \\
    &= 0.25\;[3\,\gamma(0)+2(\,\gamma(2)+2\,\gamma(1)\,)]\\
    &= 0.25\;[3\gamma(0)+2\gamma(2)+4\gamma(1)] \\
    &= 1.7571
\end{align*}

\end{enumerate}


